hi there steve kaufman  uh thank you for all the questions i'm  uh continuing my series of uh frequently  asked questions  on uh in language learning uh the first  question that was asked was  how do i deal with cases in russian or  verb endings  do i just memorize them there were other  questions about  learning you know minor languages or  what else was there i can't remember but  i'll deal with those  later so we're talking here about the  issue of case  ending noun endings or adjectives case  endings  and verb endings in russian but i think  it has application  for verb endings in other languages  romance languages and so forth  so i want to deal with this issue in  three videos  the first video will talk about the  basic concepts  the second video will talk about how one  deals with the actual  endings themselves and the third video  deals with how we get  the brain to create new neural networks  so that it will deal  with this phenomenon naturally okay  uh so with regard to concepts the basic  concept  say in russian that the endings of nouns  and adjectives  change depending on the role the  function  of the noun or adjective that's an easy  concept  so in other words whereas in english we  have prepositions  on the book in the book from the book  by the book in russian the actual word  book knight might be knee gu  knight whatever it might be  so that the the noun changes and so you  have to get your  clue about what's happening by changing  the ending of the noun and similarly  when you speak you have to change the  ending of the noun  so that's a concept that we can kind of  come to grips with  however the problem occurs when we  realize that there are so many  exceptions  for example the feminine nouns like  knight book if i read the book it's  which is then the accusative or the  direct object  and then the genitive changes kiniki and  so forth  whereas in the masculine the nominative  and the neuter the nominative  and the accusative are the same uh and  the genitive changes  however if it's an animate noun like a  person or an animal  then the accusative is the same as the  genitive  not the same as the nominative okay a  minor point if that's the only thing you  had to learn  that would be easy but when you discover  then  that uh you know if you are dealing with  one of something it's the nominative two  three or four of something  it's the genitive singular and five and  more  is the genitive plural and there are  little exceptions there depending on  whether it's masculine and feminine  the complexity starts to increase pretty  quickly  i mean i have a book here if i read you  some of the explanations you know  a b c d e f and g of  uh noun endings or aspects of verbs it's  it's  i can assure you it's tremendously  complex so the concepts  to my mind are something you can read  you have the impression you're learning  something  it's forcing you to think a little bit  about the language but you're unlikely  to make much sense of of the concepts i  mean i've been at russian for  four years and now when i read some of  these explanations  it does help me but it's taken me over a  thousand hours of listening  before these explanations actually help  me  so my advice with the concepts is get  the general  overall picture don't get too hung up  about learning the specific explanations  of what happens in this situation and  that and here's the exception and so  forth and so on because it's a thankless  task  in my opinion recognize that you will  get better at  understanding these concepts when you  have been exposed to a lot  of content through listening and reading  so that's the end of the first part  which is dealing with the  concepts
 

